db/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1039 "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1045 "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ec8f8b4c-f624-58ca-8d08-26dd958651aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115513 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56023ec9-5ce1-5f4b-8f47-3c4d6d6b2dd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115513 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty )$ .\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{1+s}$ is  $s\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3a/3ac2933afc460e42ad5bffb8b01b9fc3ff9275ab3bcf2ac3ab710a8094b36637.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is\n\n$\\,$ $s\\in (-1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1061 44256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c2c6552-011f-50fb-b06a-06bc2262ccf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1061 44257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a07d4a92-4626-5d50-92cc-ea8d55c5707d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1061 44297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cfc9c5f1-5579-598f-a9e2-d23b659763f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1061 44801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3947d2bf-1e5f-5028-8a1b-43cfaacc4546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1064 "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1065 7628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276280 #edb/ref 1080 "s\\in (-1,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "590ce956-fa57-52c8-b676-c7a081a32b28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276755 #edb/ref 1080 "s\\in (-\\infty ,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276755 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d377bcc-370f-51f9-b994-f8649f51617b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276802 #edb/ref 1080 "s\\in (0,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "928012b6-5d37-57e3-ab1c-585b2c67c370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277186 #edb/ref 1080 "s\\in (-1,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c07039d3-aec7-5e92-9950-c14bc1351f86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277576 #edb/ref 1080 "s\\in (-1,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eee05247-e7