 17592186420089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420091 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420091 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420091 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420091 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "02673689-46fa-584e-88ff-1a1c967a1b0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420092 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨{e}^{-t},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a104075-f533-58ab-b79b-2a5e66b667e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420092 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\frac{1}{t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a5/a55ef2c927b26c9beba9b44f669c817cf749b79de163b4bf0505d15c6ec31630.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420093 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420093 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420093 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420093 #edb/ref 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420093 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8302ddf-29cf-570b-b7dd-dab545b3b824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420093 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${e}^{t-1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
